// std.core.drop
//
// RAII. The compiler calls `drop` when a binding leaves scope, in
// reverse declaration order, exactly once — and after the block's
// defers have run, because a defer is registered on the block and a
// drop belongs to the value.
//
// Exactly-once is why `consume` exists: a Drop value cannot be
// copied, since both copies would drop. Want a second one?
// Construct a second one — there is no Clone.
//
// Two consequences that look like they bite and do not:
//
//   - a handle is not a Drop value. An Alloc is a fat value
//     pointing at an arena, so it copies freely; the ARENA is Drop.
//     That is why Vec can store `alloc: Alloc` by value.
//   - passing a Drop value to a parameter is a borrow, not a move.
//     A receiver is just the first parameter, so nothing else could
//     be true. A move is spelled `consume`, at the call site, and
//     only there.
//
// The allocator is the flagship user: env.mem.alloc() hands back an
// arena, and dropping the arena releases every page it ever handed
// out, in one shot.
//
// Until stage 3 nothing checks any of this, and the stdlib honours
// exactly-once by hand. That is precisely why the `consume` syntax
// ships at stage 0 unchecked: code written without it has to be
// revised, code written with it loses nothing.
Drop* = {
    // required (`= sig`, no body): an impl must provide it.
    //
    // `::` because drop writes the receiver's own bytes. The
    // compiler-inserted call is exempt from the receiver rule —
    // scope exit runs drop on `:` bindings too, because destroying
    // a value is not mutating it through a binding.
    drop* = (self :: @Self) ()
}
